<!--
    kuna GH-641: "floating point associativity" (reported with a hand-written
    x86-64 fixture and a native-execution witness).
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/641

    CPUI_FLOAT_MULT and CPUI_FLOAT_ADD pushed the same MULTIPLY / BINARY_PLUS
    tokens integer arithmetic uses, and those are marked associative, so the
    printer dropped the parentheses around a same-token right operand.  Both
    groupings a compiler can emit then printed as one flat chain: `a * b * c`
    for the `a * (b * c)` the p-code computes as well as for the `(a * b) * c`
    it computes elsewhere.  The two round differently (the reporter's pair
    returns 0x4103a6a4 and 0x4103a6a3 from the same arguments), so one of the
    two renderings was C that computes a different value than the function it
    came from.

    Stage model: P9 emit, the operator-token table and the parentheses()
    binary case (printc.rs, printlanguage.rs).

    This is a kuna DEFAULT-FLIP, like GH-2786: emitting an expression that
    re-reads as a different computation is an unambiguous bug, so the fix is
    unconditional rather than behind an option, and the usual
    bug-reproduces-by-default pass is impossible by design.  Assertions #1/#3
    prove the fixed form; #2/#4 prove the flat chain now belongs to exactly one
    of the two functions, where before this change it matched both.

    Bytes: four register-only leaf functions, assembled here.
      right_grouped: mulss %xmm2,%xmm1; mulss %xmm1,%xmm0; ret
      left_grouped:  mulss %xmm1,%xmm0; mulss %xmm2,%xmm0; ret
      right_sum:     addss %xmm2,%xmm1; addss %xmm1,%xmm0; ret
      left_sum:      addss %xmm1,%xmm0; addss %xmm2,%xmm0; ret
-->
